"[2607.08095] zkComposer: Decomposing Proof Construction to Scale zkML (https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.08095)\nciteturn1view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.08095\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 167\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Cryptography and Security\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 9 Jul 2026]\nL9: # Title:zkComposer: Decomposing Proof Construction to Scale zkML\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Pawan Kumar Sanjaya】, 【6†Christina Giannoula】, 【7†Valdy Oktavian】, 【8†Mehdi Saeedi】, 【9†Gabor Sines】, 【10†Gururaj Saileshwar】, 【11†Nandita Vijaykumar】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled zkComposer: Decomposing Proof Construction to Scale zkML, by Pawan Kumar Sanjaya and 6 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【12†View PDF】 【13†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:Zero-knowledge machine learning (zkML) enables a server to perform verifiable inference while keeping model parameters private from the client. However, existing zkML systems incur prohibitive proof-generation costs. We observe that proof generation exhibits limited parallelism; that is, prover time does not decrease significantly as the number of threads increases.\nL17: This limitation is because existing systems rely on monolithic proof computation, constructing a single proof for the entire machine learning model. We introduce zkComposer, a modular proof-construction framework that unlocks an additional dimension of parallelism, in addition to the parallelism in existing proof kernels.\nL18: zkComposer decomposes the zkML proof of correct inference into independent sub-proofs, each covering a subset of the computation for inference e.g., each independent sub-proof can cover a subset of contiguous layers in the ML model. Adjacent sub-proofs are cryptographically linked through shared commitments to the activations from the boundary layer.\nL19: zkComposer provides the same guarantees as the monolithic proof without requiring additional linking proofs or changes to the underlying cryptographic primitives. We implement zkComposer and evaluate it on three CNNs and GPT-2. We show that, on CNN workloads, zkComposer reduces prover time and response time by up to 3.25x relative to zkCNN [1]. On GPT-2, zkComposer reduces these times by up to 4.83x relative to zkGPT [2], when partitioning along the model layers.\nL20: When partitioning across both model layers and input sequences in GPT-2, we show that zkComposer reduces prover time and response time by up to 6.84x relative to zkGPT [2].\nL21: Subjects:  | Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)\nL22: Cite as:  | 【14†arXiv:2607.08095】 [cs.CR]\nL23:    | (or 【15†arXiv:2607.08095v1】 [cs.CR] for this version)\nL24:    | 【16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2607.08095†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Pawan Kumar Sanjaya [【17†view email】]\nL28: [v1] Thu, 9 Jul 2026 04:05:43 UTC (243 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled zkComposer: Decomposing Proof Construction to Scale zkML, by Pawan Kumar Sanjaya and 6 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * 【12†View PDF】\nL37:   * 【13†HTML (experimental)】\nL38:   * 【18†TeX Source】\nL39: \nL40: 【19†view license†creativecommons.org】\nL41: \nL42: ### Additional Features\nL43: \nL44:   * 【20†Audio Summary】\nL45: ### Current browse context:\nL46: \nL47: cs.CR\nL48: \nL49: 【21†< prev】   |   【22†next >】\nL50: \nL51: 【23†new】 | 【24†recent】 | 【25†2026-07】\nL52: \nL53: Change to browse by:\nL54: \nL55: 【26†cs】\nL56: \nL57: ### References & Citations\nL58: \nL59:   * 【27†SciX†scixplorer.org】\nL60:   * 【28†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL61:   * 【29†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org】\nL62: \nL63: [Button: export Bi