grange-Labs/deep-prove\nL129: \nL130: 【68†Releases】 【69†Tags】\nL131: \nL132: [Input: Find a release] [Input]\nL133: \nL134: Releases · Lagrange-Labs/deep-prove\nL135: ## There aren’t any releases here\nL136: \nL137: You can create a release to package software, along with release notes and links to binary files, for other people to use. Learn more about releases in 【70†our docs†docs.github.com】.\nL138: \nL139: You can’t perform that action at this time.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNatively Compatible Super-Efficient Lookup Arguments and How to Apply Them (https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/1058)\nciteturn1view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/1058\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 77\nL0: # What a lovely hat\nL1: #### Is it made out of 【0†tin foil†iacr.org】?\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Cryptology ePrint Archive】\nL4: \nL5:   * 【2†Papers】 Updates from the last:\nL6:     * 【3†7 days】\nL7:     * 【4†31 days】\nL8:     * 【5†6 months】\nL9:     * 【6†365 days】\nL10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * 【7†Listing by year】\nL13:     * 【8†All papers】\nL14:     * 【9†Compact view】\nL15:     * 【10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org】\nL16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * 【11†How to cite】\nL19:     * * * *\nL20:     * 【12†Harvesting metadata】\nL21:   * 【2†Submissions】\nL22:     * 【13†Submit a paper】\nL23:     * 【14†Revise or withdraw a paper】\nL24:     * 【15†Acceptance and publishing conditions】\nL25: \nL26: 【16†Image: Search Button】\nL27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2024/1058\nL30: \nL31: ### 【17†Natively Compatible Super-Efficient Lookup Arguments and How to Apply Them】\nL32: \nL33: Matteo Campanelli, Offchain Labs\nL34: \nL35: Dario Fiore, IMDEA Software\nL36: \nL37: Rosario Gennaro, The City College of New York\nL38: ##### Abstract\nL39: Lookup arguments allow an untrusted prover to commit to a vector $\\vec f \\in \\mathbb{F}^n$ and show that its entries reside in a predetermined table $\\vec t \\in \\mathbb{F}^N$. One of their key applications is to augment general-purpose SNARKs making them more efficient on subcomputations that are hard to arithmetize. In order for this \"augmentation\" to work out, a SNARK and a lookup argument should have some basic level of compatibility with respect to the commitment on $\\vec f$.\nL40: However, not all existing efficient lookup arguments are fully compatible with other efficient general-purpose SNARKs. This incompatibility can for example occur whenever SNARKs use multilinear extensions under the hood (e.g. Spartan) but the lookup argument is univariate in flavor (e.g. Caulk or $\\mathsf{cq}$).\nL41: In this paper we discuss how to widen the spectrum of \"super-efficient\" lookup arguments (where the proving time is independent of the size of the lookup table): we present a new construction inspired by $\\mathsf{cq}$and based on multilinear polynomial encodings (MLE). Our construction is the first lookup argument for any table that is also natively compatible with MLE-based SNARKs at comparable costs with other state-of-the-art lookup arguments, particularly when the large table is unstructured.\nL42: This case arises in various applications, such as using lookups to prove that the program in a virtual machine is fetching the right instruction and when proving the correct computation of floating point arithmetic (e.g., in verifiable machine learning). We also introduce a second more general construction: a compiler that, given any super-efficient lookup argument compatible with univariate SNARKs, converts it into a lookup argument compatible with MLE-based SNARKs with a very small overhead.\nL43: Finally, we discuss SNARKs that we can compose with our constructions as well as approaches for this composition to work effectively.\nL44: ##### Metadata\nL45: \nL46: Available format(s)\nL47:     【17†PDF】\nL48: Category\nL49:     【18†Cryptographic protocols】\nL50: Publication info\nL51:     Published by the IACR in JOC 2024\nL52: Keywords\nL53:     【19†lookup arguments】【20†SNARKs】【21†commit and prove】\nL54: Contact author(s)\nL55:     binarywhalesinternaryseas @ gmail com\nL56: dario fiore @ imdea org\nL57: rosario @ ccny cuny edu\nL58: History\nL59:     2024-12-06: last of 2 revisions\nL60:     2024-06-28: received\nL61:     【22†See all versions】\nL62: Short URL\nL63:     【23†https://ia.cr/2024/1058†ia.cr】\nL64: License\nL65: \nL66: 【24†CC BY†creativecommons.org】\nL67: BibTeX 【25†Image】Copy to clipboard\nL68: \nL69:     @misc{cryptoeprint:2024/1058,\nL70:           author = {Matteo Campanelli and Dario Fiore and Rosario Gennaro},\nL71:           title = {Natively Compatible Super-Efficient Lookup Arguments and How to Apply Them},\nL72:           howpublished = {Cryptology {ePrint} Archive, Paper 2024/1058},\nL73:           year = {2024},\nL74:           url = {https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/1058}\nL75:     }\nL76: Note: In order to protect the privacy of readers, eprint.iacr.org does not use cookies or embedded third party content.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUnlocking the lookup singularity with Lasso - Microsoft Research (https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/unlocking-the-lookup-singularity-with-lasso/)\nciteturn1view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/unlocking-the-lookup-singularity-with-lasso/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 98\nL0: # Unlocking the lookup singularity with Lasso\nL1: \nL2:   * 【0†Srinath Setty】 ,\nL3:   * Justin Thaler ,\nL4:   * Riad Wahby\nL5: \nL6: Eurocrypt | May 2024\nL7: \nL8: 【1†Download BibTex】\nL9: \nL10: This paper introduces Lasso, a new family of lookup arguments, which allow an untrusted prover to commit to a vector\nL11: \nL12: \\(𝑎\\in 𝐹^𝑚\\)\nL13: \nL14: and prove that all entries of a reside in some predetermined table\nL15: \nL16: \\(𝑡\\in 𝐹^𝑛\\)\nL17: . Lasso’s performance characteristics unlock the so-called “lookup singularity”. Lasso works with any multilinear polynomial commitment scheme, and provides the following efficiency properties. For\nL18: \nL19: \\(𝑚\\)\nL20: \nL21: lookups into a table of size\nL22: \nL23: \\(𝑛\\)\nL24: \nL25: , Lasso’s prover commits to just\nL26: \nL27: \\(𝑚+𝑛\\)\nL28: \nL29: field elements. Moreover, the committed field elements are small, meaning that, no matter how big the field\nL30: \nL31: \\(𝐹\\)\nL32: \nL33: is, they are all in the set\nL34: \nL35: \\({0,…,𝑚}\\)\nL36: . When using a multiexponentiation-based commitment scheme, this results in the prover’s costs dominated by only\nL37: \nL38: \\(𝑂(𝑚+𝑛)\\)\nL39: \nL40: group operations (e.g., elliptic curve point additions), plus the cost to prove an evaluation of a multilinear polynomial whose evaluations over the Boolean hypercube are the table entries. This represents a significant improvement in prover costs over prior lookup arguments (e.g., plookup, Halo2’s lookups, lookup arguments based on logarithmic derivatives).\nL41: Unlike all prior lookup arguments, if the table\nL42: \nL43: \\(𝑡\\)\nL44: \nL45: is structured (in a precise sense that we define), then no party needs to commit to\nL46: \nL47: \\(𝑡\\)\nL48: \nL49: , enabling the use of much larger tables than prior works (e.g., of size\nL50: \nL51: \\(2^{128}\\)\nL52: or larger). Moreover, Lasso’s prover only “pays” in runtime for table entries that are accessed by the lookup operations. This applies to tables commonly used to implement range checks, bitwise operations, big-number arithmetic, and even transitions of a full-fledged CPU such as RISC-V. Specifically, for any integer parameter\nL53: \nL54: \\(𝑐>1\\)\nL55: \nL56: , Lasso’s prover’s dominant cost is committing to\nL57: \nL58: \\(3\\cdot 𝑐\\cdot 𝑚+𝑐\\cdot 𝑛^{1/𝑐}\\)\nL59: field elements. Furthermore, all these field elements are “small”, meaning they are in the set\nL60: \nL61: \\({0,…,max(𝑚,𝑛^{1/𝑐},𝑞)-1}\\)\nL62: \nL63: , where\nL64: \nL65: \\(𝑞\\)\nL66: \nL67: is the maximum value in\nL68: \nL69: \\(𝑎\\)\nL70: \nL71: .\nL72: Lasso’s starting point is Spark, a time-optimal polynomial commitment scheme for sparse polynomials in Spartan (CRYPTO 2020). We first provide a stronger security analysis for Spark. Spartan’s security analysis assumed that certain metadata associated with a sparse polynomial is committed by an honest party (this is acceptable for its purpose in Spartan, but not for Lasso). We prove that Spark remains secure even when that metadata is committed by a malicious party.\nL73: This provides the first “standard” commitment scheme for sparse multilinear polynomials with optimal prover costs. We then generalize Spark to directly support a lookup argument for both structured and unstructured tables, with the efficiency characteristics noted above.\nL74: Opens in a new tab\nL75: \nL76: 【2†Publication†eprint.iacr.org】\nL77: \nL78: Follow us:\nL79: \nL80:   * 【3†Follow on X†x.com】\nL81:   * 【4†Like on Facebook†www.facebook.com】\nL82:   * 【5†Follow on LinkedIn†www.linkedin.com】\nL83:   * 【6†Subscribe on Youtube†www.youtube.com】\nL84:   * 【7†Follow on Instagram†www.instagram.com】\nL85:   * 【8†Subscribe to our RSS feed】\nL86: \nL87: Share this page:\nL88: \nL89:   * 【9†Share on X†x.com】\nL90:   * 【10†Share on Facebook†www.facebook.com】\nL91:   * 【11†Share on LinkedIn†www.linkedin.com】\nL92:   * 【12†Share on Reddit†www.reddit.com】\nL93: 【13†Surface Laptop Ultra】 【14†Surface Pro】 【15†Surface Laptop】 【16†Surface RTX Spark Dev Box】 【17†Explore Copilot】 【18†Explore Microsoft products】 【19†Windows 11 apps】 【20†Account profile†account.microsoft.com】 【21†Download Center】 【22†Microsoft Store support†go.microsoft.com】 【23†Returns】 【24†Order tracking】 【25†Certified Refurbished】 【26†Why buy from Microsoft Store】 【27†Flexible Payments】 【28†Microsoft in education】 【29†Devices for education】 【30†Microsoft Teams for Education】 【31†Microsoft 365 Edu